<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Celebrations and Traditions</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Celebrate This Tradition Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a diary entry about a significant event. Interactive: Respond to peer writing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a diary entry describing a cultural event in a coherent and organized manner (e.g., "Today, I saw a parade for Independence Day. First, the band played music. Then, the floats passed by. Finally, there were fireworks.").<br>Can respond with simple feedback to peer writing about a cultural event (e.g., "I like your story about the festival!").<br>Can write a short paragraph sharing feelings about a cultural celebration (e.g., "I am excited about celebrating Christmas with my family.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 17%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different Panamanian celebrations and traditions (e.g., a parade for Independence Day, a local festival with masks and traditional dances, people enjoying cuisine). Students briefly name the traditions they recognize and share one word describing how they feel about them (e.g., "Parade – exciting!", "Festival – fun!"). The teacher elicits relevant vocabulary like *parade, festival, dance, celebration, traditional, observe, gather, enjoy, fireworks*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a short sentence on the board: "Yesterday, I saw a festive parade. I was excited about watching the dancers." The teacher highlights 'saw' (past simple), 'festive' (adjective), 'excited about watching' (gerund after preposition), and 'dancers' (noun). The teacher models the pronunciation of 'celebration' and 'gather', emphasizing word stress, and also clarifies the /z/ sound in 'gathers' if it comes up in discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I see the parade today or yesterday?" (Expected answer: "Yesterday.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I excited FOR watching or ABOUT watching?" (Expected answer: "About watching.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt: A sample diary entry (around 80-100 words) written by a teenager about a national celebration, describing key events and personal feelings. It uses present simple, past simple, gerunds after prepositions, sequencers (first, then, finally), and target vocabulary (e.g., *independence, heritage, parade, traditional, march, enjoy, fireworks, ceremony, symbol*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing celebrations: "They decorate their homes with the national flag and participate in the parades." Expressing preferences: "My favorite tradition is the parade because..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main event the diary entry talks about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences about the diary entry and mark them T or F (e.g., "The celebration was boring. [F]", "People gathered to watch the fireworks. [T]").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match sequencers (First, Next, Finally) with the events described in the diary entry.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences and paragraphs using Present simple, Past simple, Present perfect, Gerunds after prepositions, sequencers (first, next, finally), and vocabulary related to celebrations and traditions (e.g., *celebrate, dance, gather, enjoy, march, observe, tradition, custom, parade, festival, fireworks, cultural, festive*). Practice pronunciation of irregular present tense verbs ending in third-person singular "-s," "-es," and "-ies," focusing on the correct sounds /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ as they write and silently rehearse sentences (e.g., 'celebrates' /s/, 'gathers' /z/, 'wishes' /ɪz').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences related to celebrations using the correct form of verbs (past simple, present simple, or present perfect) and suitable prepositions/gerunds.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Panama ______ (celebrate) Fiestas Patrias on November 3rd and 4th." (Present simple)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Last year, I ______ (observe) a traditional dance for the first time." (Past simple)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People _______ (participate) in parades, traditional dances, and music festivals for many years." (Present perfect)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I am excited ______ (celebrate) Christmas with my family." (Gerund after preposition)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled - Guided Paragraph Writing:</b> Students work individually to write a short paragraph (3-4 sentences) describing a family tradition using provided sentence starters and sequencers.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My family celebrates _________."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, we _______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next, we ______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Finally, I feel ______ about ______."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction - Peer Sentence Check:</b> Students exchange their guided paragraphs from activity 2 with a partner. They check for the use of at least one past simple verb and one gerund after a preposition. They give simple feedback like, "I like your story!" or "You used 'celebrated' correctly." This also allows for sociolinguistic practice in responding to peer writing.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual Written Product – A Diary Entry.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will write a diary entry (70-90 words) describing a significant cultural event or celebration they have experienced or know about in their community. The entry should address the theme "I Celebrate This Tradition Because..." by including personal feelings and reasons for the celebration's importance. They must use at least two examples of past simple, two examples of present simple, one gerund after a preposition, and two sequencers (first, next, finally). They should also integrate at least five vocabulary words from the list (e.g., *tradition, celebration, parade, festival, dance, enjoy, gather, cultural, festive, fireworks*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher provides a checklist for students to self-monitor: "Did I describe the event clearly? Did I use sequencers? Did I include my feelings? Did I use past and present verbs correctly?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a simplified rubric checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their diary entries with a different partner. Each student uses a checklist to provide feedback on their peer's writing. The checklist focuses on the learning outcome elements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Did the entry describe a cultural event clearly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Did the writer use at least two sequencers (e.g., First, Then, Finally)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Did the writer express feelings about the celebration? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Can you identify at least 3 vocabulary words from the list? (List them: _____)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Give one positive comment about the diary entry. (e.g., "I like your story about the festival!")</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper before leaving:
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One thing I learned today about writing a diary entry is...</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One word to describe how I feel about writing in English is...</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What part of writing the diary entry was most challenging for you? Why?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What did you enjoy most about sharing your tradition or celebration in writing?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully used past simple and sequencers. However, some struggled with correctly forming gerunds after prepositions and integrating present perfect. Adjustments for next lesson: provide more explicit examples and practice on gerund usage and sentence variety. Some students need more confidence in sharing personal experiences; creating a safe, encouraging environment is key. Scaffolds for next lesson could include sentence frames for expressing feelings or more guided planning for their writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their diary entries orally or visually, perhaps creating a simple poster or presenting their celebration to the class, thereby consolidating their understanding and applying their writing into a mediated output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>